I've written `plan/research/03-meta-models.md`. It runs about 6,700 words counted with `wc`, over your 2–5k target, because of the code blocks and tables. Nothing has been tested with a real key: every API fact comes from Meta's docs or the Muse files on this Mac, and no paid call was made.

**Two settings on this Mac need attention before the app uses Muse:**
- **Muse would bill your subscription, not your key.** Muse is logged in with your browser/subscription account. If `META_API_KEY` isn't set, its usage lands on the subscription.
- **Contributor mode is on.** Your Muse settings have it enabled. Contributor content can be used by Meta for training, and Meta's terms forbid sending personal information to it. Screen recordings are personal information.

The plan handles both: the app always passes the key as `META_API_KEY`, plus `--contributor-mode off` and an explicit `--model`. It also blocks any `-contributor` model in code. It never changes your Muse config files.

**API facts the app will rely on:**
- **Base URL is `https://api.meta.ai/v1`**, with `Authorization: Bearer <key>` (keys start `LLM_`). It accepts OpenAI Responses, Chat Completions and Anthropic Messages formats at the same price.
- **Muse uses the same base URL**, so one key covers Muse and the app's direct calls, with one bill and one shared rate limit.
- **Exact request shapes are in the doc:** image input (`input_image`), strict JSON-schema output (`text.format`), the rate-limit headers (`x-ratelimit-*`, `Retry-After`) and the error codes.

**Claims in BRIEF.md:**
- **True:** Muse Wonder, Muse Glimmer (an open-weight 30B model that runs locally) and SAM 3.1 are all real. Muse context windows are about 1M tokens.
- **False:** `dev.meta.ai/v1` and `llama.developer.meta.ai/v1` are not API addresses; the old Llama API was retired in July 2026. There is no `@meta/llama` npm package. Llama 4 Scout's long context doesn't help with hours of screenshots: 8 hours at one frame every 5 seconds is about 8M tokens.

**Terms that shape the product:**
- Standard (non-contributor) models are not trained on your data, but Meta publishes no retention period, and stored responses are on by default, so the app turns that off.
- You can't share your API key. If you ship the app to other people, each would need their own key, which I recommend, or you'd have to run a server in between.

**Recommended model routing:**

| Stage | Model |
|---|---|
| (a) Per-frame understanding | No model: accessibility tree, on-device OCR and events |
| (b) PII redaction | Local rules only; nothing leaves the Mac |
| (c) Episode summaries | `muse-spark-1.3`, low effort, at most 2 redacted keyframes |
| (d) Pattern mining | Local candidate mining, then nightly `muse-spark-1.3` and a weekly `muse-wonder-1.0` pass, text only |
| (e) Building micro-apps | Muse Code on `muse-wonder-1.0`, falling back to `muse-spark-1.3` |
| (f) Repairs | Rule-based fix first, then `muse-spark-1.3`, then `muse-wonder-1.0` |

- **SAM:** not useful here; the accessibility tree gives better information for free.
- **Running Glimmer locally:** an optional mode for Macs with 64 GB or more, the same limit Muse sets for its own local mode. It needs about 19 GB of memory.
- **Cost:** about $1.45 per active user-day, roughly $32 a month. A lean setup is about $0.50/day; a heavy building day is $5–8. These rest on my assumptions about usage, which are listed in the doc.

The doc also covers the design for swapping models or providers. It ends with a list of what's verified and what isn't, a first test costing under $1 to settle the open points (including whether `muse serve` picks up the key), five questions for you, and the decisions I made. I didn't touch any other agent's files.
